                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    a_1 + a_2 = k
                    \\,.
            %p
              This gives a
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
          %p
            For more along these lines see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+the+2-sphere"} geometric quantization of the 2-sphere
          %p Generally:
          %p
          #SpinStructureOnTheNSphere.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} n-sphere
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              = succeed "-realization" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n \simeq Spin(n+1)/Spin(n)
              (
              %a{:href => "sphere#LabelCosetSpaceStructure"}> here
              ), as a special case of the canonical
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
              (
              %a{:href => "G-structure#CanonicalHStructureOnFModH"}> this example
              ).
          %p
            For other ways to see this see:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gutt86"} Gutt 1986
            %a{:href => "#Nowaczyk15"} Nowaczyk 2015, Theorem A.6.6 in
          %h3#OverAKahlerManifold Over Hermitian/Kähler manifolds manifold
          %p More generally:
          #SpinstructureOnCompactKaehlerIsSquareRootOfCanonical.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A spin structure on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+manifold"} Hermitian manifold
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"}> Kähler manifold
              )
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              exists precisely if, equivalently
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  there is a choice of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo ,
                                %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
                  of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+line+bundle"} canonical line bundle
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{n,0}
                  (a “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"}> Theta characteristic
                  ”);
              %li
                %p
                  there is a trivialization of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi T
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(T X)
                  of the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
          %p In this case one has:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mo •
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi X
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S_X \simeq \Omega^{0,\bullet}_X \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}_X}
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_X
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+complex"} Dolbeault complex
              with the corresponding
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"} Theta characteristic
            %p
              Moreover, the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault-Dirac+operator"} Dolbeault-Dirac operator
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mo ∂
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\partial} + \overline{\partial}^\ast
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Hitchin74"}> Hitchin 74
            ). A textbook account is for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Friedrich74"}> Friedrich 74, around p. 79 and p. 82
            ).
          %h3#QuantumAnomaly As quantum anomaly cancellation condition
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            the existence of a spin structure on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            arises notably as the condition for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            cancellation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            for the spinning particle – the superparticle – propagating on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            It is the generalization of this anomaly computation from the worldlines of superparticles to super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"}> string
            s that leads to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+structure"} fivebrane structure
          %h2#Higher Higher spin structures
          %p
            Spin structures are one step in a tower of conditions that are related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            cancellation of higher dimensional spinning/super
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
          %p
